42: 
43: ## (a) The access-width cells
44: 
45: Grade: VD1 is floor; cons_eq2, bepop_ge4 and lepop_ge4 are disclosed debt. The width `len` is an input the handler tests directly at :1255 (`len == 4`, `len >= 2`) and at the pop sites; the 2026-10-09 derived-input ruling attaches to the computed local `consumed`, not to those tests, so it does not make the width cells all debt. The tests separate three cases: {1}, {2,3,5,6,7,8}, {4}. VD1 changes only the width-1 case, whose sole row cannot see the change -- floor, one count read fixes it. cons_eq2 and the pops change behaviour only at widths 3 and 5-8, inside the bucket the handler does not split from width 2 -- debt, written down with the width-8 delta.
46: 
47: Can widths 3/5/6/7 reach the data register? No, on any CPU in this tree (READ). The CPUs that carry wdc issue 1/2/4/8-byte accesses only: MIPS lb/lh/lw and lld/ld/sd (cpu_mips_instr.c:2932-2953), and the unaligned ldl/ldr helper reads the ALIGNED 8-byte word (cpu_mips_instr_unaligned.c:46-47, :88-89); PPC lfd, Alpha ldq, M88K ld.d and ARM ldrd are 8. The device-length clamp (memory_rw.c:495-523) shortens only an access that starts PAST offset 0, which is a different register, not the data register. The 1 KB-granule split at memory_rw.c:183-226 applies to translated accesses only and can hand the device a short chunk only if the data register sits at the top of a granule (offset 0x3f9-0x3ff) or at the bottom of one (offset 0) with a successful head chunk from the bytes below it. The wdc bases are 0x1f0/0x170 (ISA and PCI IDE), 0x900001f0 (arc), 0x14000180 (hpcmips) -- none near a granule edge -- and 0x14000000 (landisk, 1 KB-aligned), whose CPU is SH-4: it issues no 8-byte access (an 8-byte fmov is two 4-byte memory_rw calls, cpu_sh_instr.c:798), traps misaligned ones (SH_ALIGN_CHECK_LD, :774), and has nothing mapped immediately below the device for a head chunk to succeed in. Width 8 does reach the register (MIPS lld/ld/sd at the 8-aligned 0x1f0 on arc, evbmips, cobalt, algor), which is why cons_eq2 and the pops are debt with a real delta rather than out of contract.
48: 
49: ## (b) The CMDOUT occupancy cell and D2_and_st
50: 
51: Yes: it is a coverage row the next repair owes. The dispatch fires only at occupancy exactly 12 (:1717), and a run of stores can step over it (2, 6, 10, 14, then 2-byte stores to 514) without ever dispatching -- f4_run.txt confirms `st=NULL phase=0x9 dispatched=no` at occupancy 514 with every store driven through the real handler. One width-2 read then pops two bytes before the flag test (occupancy 512), and the PIO branch's 512-boundary test fires. HEAD, with the flag set, takes the transfer-accounting branch, latches warned_null_xfer and raises no interrupt; D2_and_st, finding atapi_st NULL, takes the PIO branch and raises one (`sink=1`). The operand it swaps in, atapi_st, is read by the repaired refill (:1424, :1461), so (flag x atapi_st) is a floor joint -- the coordinator already grades its (flag 0, non-NULL) cells floor (Rows 1 and 2) -- and this is its (flag 1, NULL) cell. No existing row builds that occupancy (the one flag-1/NULL row reads an empty ring and checks only that the process did not terminate), which is why the variant went undetected 159/159. Row N5: enter CMDOUT, drive occupancy to 514 through data-port stores, one width-2 read, want no interrupt and the latch set. It detects D2_and_st and D2_st on this cell; D2_or_st, D2_phase and the equivalents match HEAD here (f4_run.txt), so it cannot redden an unchanged row. Without it the next repair closes half a joint and the pass after finds the other half -- the pattern the repair-chain rule exists to stop.
52: 
53: ## Which coordinator disposition is wrong
54: 
55: F4 (with D7). The coordinator described D2_and_st by the field that co-varies -- the warning latch -- instead of by the operand that was swapped -- atapi_st, which the repair reads -- and so graded a floor cell as equivalent. The same discriminator, applied correctly to F10/F11/F18/F19 (latches the repair does not read), gives debt; applied to F4 it gives floor. The measurement in f4_run.txt settles it. Every other accepted disposition rests on a fact I read (the result-code domain, the flag/phase co-writers, the width reachability, the drain's write_in_progress gate, status_byte's 0x50) or on a control that matched HEAD in the same run (D2_phase, D2_or_st).
