17: ================================================================================
18: THE TWO QUESTIONS THE ADJUDICATORS SPLIT ON
19: ================================================================================
20: 
21: ## Q2 of the packet: F4 -- can a CMDOUT ring sit so the PIO 512-boundary fires before any dispatch? MEASURED: YES.
22: 
23: **Answer: YES, and therefore D2_and_st HAS a guest INTRQ delta -- F4 is NOT equivalent.**
24: 
25: The dispatch fires only at `inbuf_len == 12` EXACTLY (dev_wdc.c:1717), and the ring is empty on entry to CMDOUT (wdc_new_command_reset flushes it at :923, run unconditionally at :938 before ATAPI_PKT_CMD sets flag+CMDOUT at :1046-1047). But a guest can STEP PAST 12 without landing on it and keep pushing: a 2-byte store then three 4-byte stores give occupancy 2 -> 6 -> 10 -> 14 (the exact stride the packet's question names), after which the dispatch guard never matches again (occupancy only grows, `== 12` is never revisited), so 2-byte stores walk it to any even value. Occupancy 514 is reachable in CMDOUT with flag 1, atapi_st still NULL. A width-2 DATA read then pops 2 FIRST (occupancy 514 -> 512, the pops at :1198-1213 precede the flag test), and the PIO else-branch at :1468 checks `inbuf_tail != inbuf_head && (tail-head) % 512 == 0` -- 512 is a nonzero multiple of 512, so it asserts.
26: 
27: MEASURED (fable5/meas_A/f4_run.txt), driving the REAL `dev_wdc_access` for every store and the read, with the CMDOUT state produced only by what the writers produce (output confirms `st=NULL phase=0x9 dispatched=no`):
28: 
29: ```
30: HEAD       STATE occ=514 ... dispatched=no   READ int_assert=0 sink=0 warned_null_xfer=1
31: D2_st      STATE occ=514 ... dispatched=no   READ int_assert=1 sink=1 warned_null_xfer=0
32: D2_or_st   STATE occ=514 ... dispatched=no   READ int_assert=0 sink=0 warned_null_xfer=1
33: D2_and_st  STATE occ=514 ... dispatched=no   READ int_assert=1 sink=1 warned_null_xfer=0
34: D2_phase   STATE occ=514 ... dispatched=no   READ int_assert=0 sink=0 warned_null_xfer=1
35: ```
36: 
37: HEAD takes the ATAPI arm (`if (d->atapi_cmd_in_progress)`), finds atapi_len 0 and atapi_st NULL, latches `warned_null_xfer`, asserts NO interrupt. D2_and_st (`flag && atapi_st != NULL`) finds st NULL, takes the PIO arm, and asserts INTRQ at the 512-boundary. So the two differ on a GUEST channel (INTRQ: sink 1 vs 0), not merely on the host latch. F4's premise "differs ONLY in the host latch warned_null_xfer" is FALSE.
38: 
39: **Grade: FLOOR.** The read-arm flag guard (:1228) dominates the re-arm repair (clamp :1427, refill :1460). The repair path reads `d->atapi_st` (:1424 `d->atapi_st->data_in_len`, :1461 `d->atapi_st->data_in[...]`), so `atapi_st` is a repair-path lvalue and (flag x atapi_st) is in pass-11's floor cross-product -- the SAME joint that makes D2_st and D2_or_st FLOOR (coordinator agrees for those). D2_and_st pins the (flag 1, st NULL) cell of that joint; it is reachable (measured) and guest-distinguishable (measured). FLOOR. The 159-row table scores it 0/159 (SCORES.txt), i.e. it ESCAPES; no existing row builds the occ-514-CMDOUT state (the only (flag 1, st NULL) row, `row_null_atapi_st`, does one read on an EMPTY ring in DATAIN and forks checking only WIFSIGNALED). This is exactly the #499 shape the repair-chain amendment warns of: the coordinator's N1/N2 close the (flag 0, *) half of the joint and leave the (flag 1, NULL) half open for the next pass.
40: 
41: This is distinct from F18/F19 (the `|| warned_null_xfer` escapes). Those swap the flag to `warned_null_xfer`, which is NOT read by the repair path, so they are (e) by the repair-path bound (the coordinator's grade stands). D2_and_st swaps to `atapi_st`, which IS read by the repair path -> FLOOR. The coordinator conflated the two by describing F4 in terms of the latch; the operand is the discriminator.
42: 
43: ## Q1 of the packet: D3 -- the width `len` grade and reachability of widths 3,5,6,7. Ruling: (iii), the coordinator's reading, with a firmer warrant.
44: 
45: `len` is a FUNCTION INPUT the guarded body tests directly (dev_wdc.c:1255 `consumed = len == 4 ? 4 : (len >= 2 ? 2 : 1)`, and the pop tests at :1201/:1203/:1208/:1210). The 2026-10-09 derived-input ruling (carrier :1598-1608) governs `consumed` -- the derived LOCAL, graded by what the body tests on IT (`consumed > atapi_len`) -- and it does NOT demote the tests the body performs on the input `len` itself. So reading (ii) is wrong: the derived-input ruling does not make the width mutants all (e).
46: 
47: The body-induced partition on `len` (for the consume/pop logic, which is all that reaches a guest channel; the width-8 debug-format branch at :1222 is a no-op `debug()`) is `{1}, {2,3,5,6,7,8}, {4}` -- the ternary's three arms, with width 8 BUCKETED WITH width 2 (both give consumed 2 and pop 2 at HEAD). That is the case-partition reading (iii).
48: 
49: **Reachability of widths 3, 5, 6, 7 at the WDC data port: NONE, on any CPU here (READ).** Every CPU that carries wdc issues only widths 1, 2, 4, 8 at a device data port:
50: - MIPS (arc R4000/R4400, evbmips 5Kc, cobalt/algor RM5200 -- the ISA and PCI IDE carriers): `lb/lh/lw` = 1/2/4; `lld/ld/sd` issue `sizeof(uint64_t)` = 8 (cpu_mips_instr.c:2932, :2952). `ldl/ldr` go through mips_unaligned_loadstore with `wlen=8` but issue the ALIGNED 8-byte word to the device (cpu_mips_instr_unaligned.c:46-47, 88), never a 3-byte access.
51: - PPC `lfd/stfd/ld` = 8, Alpha `ldq/stq` = 8, M88K `ld.d` = 8, ARM `ldrd` = 8 -- all power-of-two.
52: - The device-length clamp (memory_rw.c:495 `paddr + len > device.length`) can only PRODUCE an odd width if an access starts PAST wd_data (device offset > 0), which lands on a different register (`case wd_err` etc.), not `case wd_data`. wd_data is device offset 0.
53: - The MEMORY_RW split (:183-226) fires at the 1024-byte granule for TRANSLATED accesses only, and its head "can never be a device" (:160, device dispatch `goto do_return_ok`s first). At every real wdc base (0x1f0, 0x170, 0x14000000, 0x900001f0) wd_data (offset 0) is 1024-aligned or far from a 1024 boundary, so an 8-byte read does not straddle; and a straddle's tail chunk starts at device offset >= 8 (>= length), i.e. not this device. So no 3/5/6/7-byte access reaches wd_data.
54: 
55: Width 8 IS reachable (MIPS `lld/ld/sd` on the MIPS wdc carriers), confirming GA's `lld` reading and the #337 comment at dev_wdc.c:1597.
56: 
57: **Ruling (iii):**
58: - **VD1 (`consumed = len == 4 ? 4 : 2`) -> FLOOR.** It differs from HEAD ONLY at width 1 (widths 2,3,4,5,6,7,8 already consume 2 or 4). Width 1 is the BELOW-the-bound region of `len >= 2` (rule D2, carrier :1538-1550), and the body's own third ternary arm -- a distinguished case. The only width-1 reader (`write route: 1-byte store is refused`, 32768 width-1 reads) has an oracle of occupancy + a constant 0xc3 fill, blind to the consumed change. A floor cell with a blind row. UPHELD (my FR graded it FLOOR; I uphold it harder below).
59: - **cons_eq2 and bepop_ge4/lepop_ge4 -> (e) DISCLOSED.** cons_eq2 (`len >= 2` -> `len == 2`) and the pops (`len == 4` -> `len >= 4`) differ ONLY at widths {3,5,6,7,8}. Widths 3,5,6,7 are unreachable; width 8 is reachable but BUCKETED with width 2 (not a case the body distinguishes from 2), so it is a value inside the `{2,3,5,6,7,8}` bucket -- clause-(e) coverage debt with a named width-8 delta, exactly as the contract already discloses for `n_C6`/`n_E_cons_ge4`. The far (past-bound) region of `len >= 2` has its BRANCH discharged by the width-2 rows; a width-8 row would only pin the branch, and there is no further bound to pin. So no floor row is owed.
60: 
61: The coordinator wrote it was "least sure of this ruling of any." It is sound: the derived-input ruling attaches to `consumed`, not to `len`; width 1 is a genuine below-bound/case cell with a blind row (floor); the width-8 delta is real (reachable) but bucketed (disclosed). One OPTIONAL width-8 read row would collapse cons_eq2, both pops, n_C6 and n_E_cons_ge4 at once (GA, kimi substitute) -- worth noting as cheap coverage, but NOT floor-owed.
